WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:24.470
Hello.

9
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:25.310
Hi.

10
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.630
Guess what we're talking about today?

11
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:28.510
Woof.

12
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.210
Woof.com.

13
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.390
Oh, my gosh.

14
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.250
Ryan's startup company is here.

15
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.170
This is Season 7, Episode 9.

16
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.890
It was written by Aaron Schur and directed by Danny Liener.

17
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:43.750
He was a new director for us.

18
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:44.090
Yeah.

19
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.330
He had directed Dude, Where's My Car?

20
00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.110
and Harold and Kumar Go to White Castle.

21
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:49.270
We had a lot of fun.

22
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:49.750
We did.

23
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.770
Here is your summary.

24
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.830
Ryan and Michael's business venture, Woof.com, has hit a snag.

25
00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:01.530
And their investors slash coworkers are pressuring them to sell the company.

26
00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.010
Sell the company?

27
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.050
The company is days old.

28
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:05.850
I know.

29
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.730
It's hit a snag so quickly.

30
00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.390
There's a lot of graphs for it, though.

31
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.030
So many color copies.

32
00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.830
Oh, I love that runner.

33
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.810
I don't like to be a lousy snitch.

34
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:23.050
Well, in the meantime, there's a new corporate policy, ye olde commission cap.

35
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.090
And Jim has hit his commission cap, so he no longer feels motivated to work.

36
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:37.950
And finally, Dwight is going to relive his childhood by turning the parking lot into a hay festival, which leads Angela into the arms of a new man.

37
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:38.750
Oh, yeah.

38
00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:40.170
You know what?

39
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.370
This was our Thanksgiving episode, I realized.

40
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.450
That's why there's a hay festival.

41
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.050
Aw.

42
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.630
This aired on November 18th, 2010.

43
00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.990
We're always asking, where's our Thanksgiving episode?

44
00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:51.990
I think this was it.

45
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.670
Here you go.

46
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:53.670
Woof.com.

47
00:01:53.910 --> 00:02:01.370
Well, we're really excited because today we are going to talk to our writer of this episode and friend, Aaron Scherr.

48
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:02.050
Yes.

49
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.670
Aaron was so lovely, and we got to talk to him for quite a bit.

50
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.310
He was such a great gueSt.

51
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.750
Jenna, you and I always love it when writers get to come on.

52
00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.230
Well, we're such nerds for the writing process, I think.

53
00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:12.530
We are.

54
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.830
Well, here's our conversation with Aaron.

55
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:19.880
Hi.

56
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:20.900
Woo-hoo.

57
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.940
Everyone, we're so excited.

58
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:32.840
We're being joined by our writer, Aaron Scherr, who, by the way, was nominated for a Writer's Guild Award for episodic comedy for this episode, Woof.com.

59
00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.660
That was one of the high points of my career was getting that nomination.

60
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.020
Well, we loved this episode.

61
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:38.700
Woof.

62
00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.340
I was cracking up.

63
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.300
I hadn't seen this episode in so long.

64
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.600
I know we always say that because we are doing a rewatch in real time.

65
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.640
But I was cracking up.

66
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:48.700
Yeah, it was fun.

67
00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.080
I really enjoyed the chance to revisit it.

68
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.540
And the number of person hours that went into it, crazy.

69
00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.260
25 pages of notes from Jonathan Hughes and Kelly Hannon, the writing assistants.

70
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:01.080
Wow.

71
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:08.100
And then we, you know, whittled that down to a beat sheet and then to an outline and then an initial draft.

72
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.320
And then a lot changed after the table draft.

73
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.900
The game character, Zach Woods.

74
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.040
You know, normally when you're a writer, people will be like,

75
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.720
how much do the actors make up?

76
00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.160
And it's, you know, actors usually bring so much more to it than just coming up with lines, right?

77
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.620
But it's almost never as much improv as people think.

78
00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.940
You're like, usually it's more or less what we wrote.

79
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:28.370
Yeah, right.

80
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:35.140
And they come up with, you know, they will add things, especially brilliant improvisers like Steve Carell are going to bring new stuff.

81
00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.340
But generally speaking, we do the writing and they do the acting.

82
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.700
But Zach kind of improvised that whole thing about being in the men's room and looking away.

83
00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.000
Yes, I remember that.

84
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.760
And John couldn't keep a straight face.

85
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:47.020
No.

86
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.180
It's that scene where John is complaining about the commission cap.

87
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.580
And then Gabe gives this long analogy about how, well, sometimes there are regulations you don't agree with.

88
00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:01.820
Like men are allowed to walk around naked at my gym and I don't like it, but it's, you know, that's their policy.

89
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.340
I looked in the script because I was like, that sounds like Zach.

90
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.000
He would go on these just bizarre tangents.

91
00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.260
He's a brilliant improviser.

92
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:12.740
Yes.

93
00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.720
And that, that was an amazing speech.

94
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.620
Well, let's take you a little bit back to the beginning.

95
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.140
How long did you work as a writer on The Office?

96
00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.820
Well, I was there five, four seasons.

97
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.660
Wait, five, six, seven, eight.

98
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:27.600
So four seasons.

99
00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.240
The, the season when the, the Scranton Strangler was around too.

100
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.340
That's just coincidental.

101
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.400
I wasn't, I wasn't the Strangler.

102
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:43.820
Well, before you worked on The Office, you worked on Everybody Loves Raymond and The New Adventures of Old Christine.

103
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.380
Those were both three camera shows in front of a live audience.

104
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:53.780
I'm curious, what was the difference between working on shows like that versus working on The Office?

105
00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.080
Which was single camera.

106
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:55.640
Yeah.

107
00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.720
Well, the writing part is fairly similar.

108
00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.740
The biggest difference is in the production.

109
00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:08.560
So when you're comparing single camera to multicam, it was my first single camera show.

110
00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:15.620
And one of the things is, you know, a multicam is shot like a, like a stage production.

111
00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:21.820
Like you're watching a play and usually, ideally there's a real audience there enjoying it and you can't get that energy.

112
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.560
Whereas our show, The Office was, you know, shot like a movie and the camera is a character.

113
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:38.060
And so you're in the space and, and it's much tighter and like people are, are speaking more quietly and more really, realistically.

114
00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:48.120
And, but for me being on the set, I was used to laughing loudly to try to encourage the, the actors and make it clear what parts were the joke.

115
00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.600
And so I had to learn not to do that.

116
00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:58.960
I remember my first episode was Baby Shower and Greg Daniels was directing and he had to keep telling me like, Hey, don't, don't, don't laugh.

117
00:05:59.120 --> 00:05:59.540
Be quiet.

118
00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:09.700
And so I'd go home and have held in some, cause oh my God, the performances were so amazing and so funny and it's so hard not to laugh.

119
00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:16.940
So I'd go home and feel all this pent up laughter and it usually come out and like, you know, indigestion and burps and stuff.

120
00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:27.260
But, but also there was sort of a generational change from Raymond to The Office, then other shows I've gone on to like People of Earth in terms of how we relate to the internet,

121
00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:30.420
which is apropos of this episode, right?

122
00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.040
Which on, on Every Bill as Raymond, we would be like, don't go to those chat boards.

123
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.240
Don't, you know, MySpace.

124
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:45.780
And then on The Office, we were, you know, interacting with Office Tally and Jenny Tan and we were kind of doing the equivalent of live streaming in that day during an episode,

125
00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.680
when the episode would air, we would, the writing staff would get on and be like tweeting.

126
00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.920
So on The Office, we would draw from the internet.

127
00:06:53.140 --> 00:07:00.400
It was, it was like a reciprocal relationship where we would talk to the internet and the internet would talk to us and we would take stuff from the internet.

128
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:06.340
Like in, you know, the wedding dance was straight off the internet and YouTube, right?

129
00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:12.460
And also lip dubs, like the opening of Nepotism, where there was a lip dub and you see woof.com in there.

130
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:21.880
Well, you know, on our show, a lot of times you guys would pitch us lines in the moment because we've said this before, the writers were on set with us.

131
00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:30.280
But my small experience with multicam is that you sort of figure out your jokes and change your jokes in the rehearsal process.

132
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.240
And there's not a lot of like on the shoot day coming up with new jokes.

133
00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.100
Is that true?

134
00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:42.760
Or did you feel pressure while we were shooting that you had to stand there and throw new lines at us?

135
00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:43.820
Yes.

136
00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:48.780
Well, we would have, you know, a candy bag with lots of alternative lines ready to go.

137
00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:53.500
We would write a lot of pages that would not end up in the final cut.

138
00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.120
They maybe would be deleted scenes.

139
00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:03.220
But on a multicam, like Everybody Loves Raymond, we pretty much had every page that we wrote would end up in the final episode.

140
00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.600
Whereas on The Office, we would lose 20, 30% of pages.

141
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:08.720
Oh, yeah.

142
00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:12.720
And that's the power of the doc style, though, because it's found footage.

143
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:20.960
It's okay if it feels like, you know, you're not seeing every single moment and you can lose a lot and still have the story be there.

144
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.940
And you can only, you're able to keep just the best moments.

145
00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:31.060
I remember once I went to a panel of aspiring writers and I showed an episode I wrote.

146
00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.020
It might have been Wolf.

147
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.060
And it was on a DVD.

148
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.200
Remember DVDs?

149
00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:35.860
Oh, yeah.

150
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:41.800
And I accidentally hit play of deleted scenes and I didn't realize it.

151
00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:47.440
And so we were watching only the deleted scenes and it had the basic shape of the entire episode.

152
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.120
I thought, wow, this is oddly edited for syndication.

153
00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:59.600
But it was so like there was enough of a like upside down world version of the episode there that we had written and shot that didn't make it, you know.

154
00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:07.900
So sometimes you would have to write scenes with enough story markers in it so that if it's the only part of this story that survives,

155
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.260
there's enough there to hold it up as a story.

156
00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.240
So there is a big flexibility in the editing, probably more so than a multicam.

157
00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:16.160
Okay.

158
00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.920
I have a question for you.

159
00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.940
Aaron, we have shared with our podcast listeners that you were the, quote, king of cold opens.

160
00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:25.800
You really were.

161
00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.060
You came up with some of the most brilliant ones.

162
00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.920
I mean, everyone knows Kevin's Chili.

163
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.260
That is like the spill heard around the world.

164
00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.960
But were there some other ones that you wrote that you would like to share with us or do you have a favorite?

165
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:47.920
So, yeah, I came up, especially season five, there was a fair number of cold opens that I kind of came up with.

166
00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.700
The PA system, I believe, was my idea.

167
00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.300
Oh, yeah.

168
00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:53.160
Yeah.

169
00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.620
And actually, that was from the offices of Everybody Loves Raymond.

170
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:04.960
We figured out that you could do that, that there was a code you could hit on any phone and you'd suddenly have the entire PA system at your disposal.

171
00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.040
So that was from, basically, I didn't come up with things.

172
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.320
I just remembered things.

173
00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.160
Cheese puffs was another thing I came up with.

174
00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:18.940
Believe it or not, I was in the kitchen and I saw the cheese puffs and I threw one into my mouth.

175
00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.040
And that's, you know, when you're a writer, that's what you do.

176
00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:22.060
You're like, is this a thing?

177
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.720
Is this, you know, I'm tapping my, I'm tapping my pencil.

178
00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:25.380
Is that a thing?

179
00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.440
You know, you're just constantly trying to, desperate to come up with ideas.

180
00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.900
Gosh, all of these are really so memorable, too.

181
00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:32.500
I know.

182
00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.540
You said cheese puffs cold open.

183
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.820
I mean, that, I get that sent to me all the time.

184
00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:42.160
People want to know if we really caught them and all that.

185
00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:48.300
You know, that was an example, though, where people throwing cheese puffs into their own mouths is not in and of itself a great idea.

186
00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.760
But what you did with it made it so delightful.

187
00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:54.500
And you were really, I mean, I just watched, I rewatched that.

188
00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:59.940
And it's so, what you did made it a thing, you know, the way you reacted to it.

189
00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:03.320
And it's also kind of impressive that you were able to do that.

190
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.320
But I feel like that little thing, it's really cool because it's so relatable.

191
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.920
We've all done that with popcorn or whatever.

192
00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:16.300
Maybe you're procrastinating or, you know, it's such a relatable thing.

193
00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:24.380
And then the other thing that's so relatable is like coming up with bigger stunts for yourself, you know, trick shots with food.

194
00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:28.400
And then the joy when you land one that you came up with.

195
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.080
That's just, that's a thing.

196
00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:30.580
Yeah.

197
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:31.940
Like you tapped into something.

198
00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:38.140
I think you're so great, Erin, at just observing and then bringing what you've observed to the page.

199
00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:46.480
And I would love to see your notes because I feel like, are you someone when you go out in the world or you travel, are you like jotting down little moments?

200
00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:46.840
Like.

201
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:47.320
Yes.

202
00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:47.760
Yes.

203
00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:50.600
A lot of three by five cards, actually.

204
00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.500
Oh, you're speaking Angela's language.

205
00:11:54.420 --> 00:12:03.300
Well, you, I know you go, you've talked about that, how much, how much we try to externalize the stories by putting them on three by five cards and putting,

206
00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:04.540
tacking them to the wall.

207
00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:09.400
And, and, you know, as you show in your book, there's all those, you know, the writer's room is just festooned with them.

208
00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:19.040
And, uh, it's a great, you know, Greg Daniels, that was always his, one of his many, uh, great writing ideas is trying to externalize it, get it out of your head,

209
00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:20.200
put it down on paper.

210
00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.080
And, and it makes, it makes it something you can literally manipulate in the world.

211
00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.820
And it also gives you a new perspective on the, on the idea.

212
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.320
So it's, um.

213
00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:34.500
Was there ever a note card you put on the wall that, that didn't make it, but you still remember it?

214
00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.140
Tons of them.

215
00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:44.640
I kept, one of them is the, I wanted there to be a, uh, if not a cold open, an entire episode where Michael actually gets rose colored glasses.

216
00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.740
Cause he's heard that expression and he wonders, you know, what it's like.

217
00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:52.120
So he literally gets a rose colored glasses and then, um, drives to work.

218
00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:52.620
And it's true.

219
00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.660
Life is much better when you have rose colored glasses.

220
00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:55.920
Cause all the lights were green.

221
00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.980
There wasn't a single red light on his way to work.

222
00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:01.820
And people were waving at him.

223
00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:03.240
Like your life is better when you.

224
00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.540
That actually sounds like an amazing cold open.

225
00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.040
Like I can just see Michael.

226
00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:07.640
Yeah.

227
00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.240
That's the thing.

228
00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.040
I fully see him doing it.

229
00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.540
Like buying them and be like, everyone's my friend.

230
00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:13.760
Everything's awesome.

231
